\chapter{Embedding MuLL in D}
The D runtime is part of the \api{MuDSL} module. The \api{MuLL} namespace-like
struct supplies convenient aliases for \api{Runtime}, \api{Value}, and
\api{Error}; the underlying public names are \api{MuLLRuntime},
\api{MuLLValue}, and \api{MuLLError}.

\begin{code}
import MuDSL;
import mull.stdlib.Stdlib;
import std.stdio : writeln;

void main() {
    auto vm = new MuLL.Runtime(["mull/stdlib"]);
    install(vm);
    vm.stdoutSink = (string s) { /* send s to the application */ };
    auto value = vm.run("return 6 * 7;", "startup.mull");
    assert(value.number() == 42);
}
\end{code}

Compile the embedding source, \api{MuDSL.d}, \api{mull/stdlib/Stdlib.d}, and
the selected \api{Native.d} modules together. For example, from the toolkit
root:
\begin{code}
ldc2 -I. examples/27-mull.d MuDSL.d mull/stdlib/Stdlib.d \
    mull/stdlib/{printf,terminal,math,text,list}/Native.d \
    -of=mull-example
\end{code}

\section{Values and callbacks}
Construct values with \api{MuLL.Value(...)}. Use \api{number},
\api{stringValue}, \api{list}, \api{object}, \api{member}, and \api{call}
to inspect and invoke them. Native callbacks are delegates taking
\api{MuLLValue[]} and returning \api{MuLLValue}. A nil result is
\api{MuLLValue.init}.

D's garbage collector retains captured environments and delegates. The
runtime still uses the same operational limits and single-threaded session
model as C++. Catch \api{MuLL.Error} to access its \api{msg}, \api{line},
and \api{column}. Application bindings and package caches persist across runs.

\section{The shared MuLIB bridge}
Both hosts can install the generated native MuLIB provider. Enable
\api{MULL_ENABLE_MULIB} in CMake and point \api{MULL_MULIB_INCLUDE_DIR} at the
MuLIB headers. C++ standard-library installation then includes the provider.
In D, compile \api{mull/stdlib/Mulib/Native.d} and \api{Bridge.d}, link
\api{libmull-mulib-bridge.a}, and call \api{installMulib}, or enable the
\api{MULL_ENABLE_MULIB} version for aggregate installation.

\begin{code}
mulib Parsec <- G:MulibWrap("Parsec")
val digit <- Parsec:Range(48, 57);
val result <- Parsec:New("result");
G:Assert(Parsec:Run(digit, "7", 1, nil, result) == 1);
Parsec:ResultFree(result);
\end{code}

Wrappers expose full C function names and short aliases, native constructors,
field access, buffers, arrays, pointer outputs, and typed callbacks. They follow
the original library's argument and ownership contracts. The runtime-scoped
storage budget defaults to 64 MiB. Native resources still require their
documented release calls. See \api{mull/stdlib/Mulib/README.md} for the complete
binding interface and regeneration procedure.
